WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.607 --> 00:00:02.132
- And don't get too worried about

00:00:02.132 --> 00:00:04.549
what other people think
of you all the time.

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:06.286
We must allow for creative failure

00:00:06.286 --> 00:00:07.950
when it comes to generating ideas.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.064
It is inevitable.

00:00:09.064 --> 00:00:10.996
That is because it is
only on our strengths

00:00:10.996 --> 00:00:13.804
that can give us the energy
to correct our weaknesses.

00:00:13.804 --> 00:00:16.170
I think the simplest thing
in dealing with disagreements

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.804
is get them onto the table.

00:00:17.804 --> 00:00:19.864
Businesses are good at getting better

00:00:19.864 --> 00:00:21.707
but poor at getting different.

00:00:21.707 --> 00:00:23.921
- He's an Indian business tycoon,

00:00:23.921 --> 00:00:25.820
investor, and philanthropist.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.267
He's informally known as the
Czar of the Indian IT Industry.

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:32.981
He has an estimated net
worth of $16.5 billion.

00:00:32.981 --> 00:00:34.727
He's Azim Premji, and her is my take

00:00:34.727 --> 00:00:36.713
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:38.389
Number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:38.389 --> 00:00:39.617
and I'm curious to figure out

00:00:39.617 --> 00:00:42.367
which one you guys like the best.

00:00:44.512 --> 00:00:46.007
- [Azim] You're asking me is

00:00:46.007 --> 00:00:47.491
what should really motivate you.

00:00:47.491 --> 00:00:48.324
- [Audience Member] Yeah.

00:00:48.324 --> 00:00:50.698
- You know, I think that's
a very personal question

00:00:50.698 --> 00:00:52.846
of what should really motivate you.

00:00:52.846 --> 00:00:55.679
I think the longest standing thing

00:00:56.775 --> 00:00:58.405
which should really motivate you

00:00:58.405 --> 00:01:01.698
is that something in which
you're trying to quest

00:01:01.698 --> 00:01:05.108
for something to satisfy yourself

00:01:05.108 --> 00:01:07.844
and feel good about yourself.

00:01:07.844 --> 00:01:09.844
And decide what that is,

00:01:11.325 --> 00:01:14.624
and be constant to that purpose,

00:01:14.624 --> 00:01:17.844
and try to work for
excellence in that area.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:19.171
And don't get too worried about

00:01:19.171 --> 00:01:22.311
what other people think
of you all the time.

00:01:22.311 --> 00:01:23.983
We must allow for creative failure

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:25.956
when it comes to generating ideas.

00:01:25.956 --> 00:01:27.671
It is inevitable.

00:01:27.671 --> 00:01:29.636
No one likes failure,

00:01:29.636 --> 00:01:31.966
particularly business leaders.

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:34.474
But unfortunately, all evidence suggests

00:01:34.474 --> 00:01:37.188
that it is impossible to
generate few good ideas

00:01:37.188 --> 00:01:39.747
without a lot of bad ideas.

00:01:39.747 --> 00:01:41.132
And bad ideas are expensive,

00:01:41.132 --> 00:01:43.473
but they have to be tolerated.

00:01:43.473 --> 00:01:47.454
As the physics Nobel
Prize winner Fermi said,

00:01:47.454 --> 00:01:50.204
"I try to fail as fast as I can."

00:01:51.631 --> 00:01:54.520
That is the best way to
move forward on a new idea,

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.909
fail as fast as you can.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:59.318
Failure should be forgiven and forgotten

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:03.118
as long as the lessons are
remembered and recycled.

00:02:03.118 --> 00:02:04.280
You must begin life,

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.289
and you must approach
life with your strengths.

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:09.812
Absolutely essential.

00:02:09.812 --> 00:02:12.187
Everyone in schooling very often

00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:16.354
keeps on pushing one to focus
on what is wrong with us,

00:02:17.717 --> 00:02:20.570
or tells us what is wrong with us.

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.953
And there is a very
interesting imaginary story

00:02:22.953 --> 00:02:26.549
of a rabbit, and you must hear this one.

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:28.971
The rabbit was enrolled
in a rabbit school,

00:02:28.971 --> 00:02:31.775
and like all rabbits,
it could hop very well

00:02:31.775 --> 00:02:33.516
but could not swim.

00:02:33.516 --> 00:02:34.717
At the end of the year,

00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:36.651
the rabbit got high marks in hopping,

00:02:36.651 --> 00:02:38.909
but failed in swimming.

00:02:38.909 --> 00:02:40.488
The parents were concerned.

00:02:40.488 --> 00:02:41.718
The relations were concerned.

00:02:41.718 --> 00:02:45.223
The friends were concerned, the
elder friends, particularly.

00:02:45.223 --> 00:02:46.374
They said, forget about hopping,

00:02:46.374 --> 00:02:48.308
you are anyway good at it.

00:02:48.308 --> 00:02:49.823
You don't have to keep practicing it.

00:02:49.823 --> 00:02:52.240
Concentrate on your swimming.

00:02:53.682 --> 00:02:55.878
They sent the rabbit for (mumbles)

00:02:55.878 --> 00:02:58.297
and coaching in swimming,

00:02:58.297 --> 00:02:59.711
and guess what happened?

00:02:59.711 --> 00:03:00.866
The rabbit forgot how to hop.

00:03:00.866 --> 00:03:02.391
(laughter)

00:03:02.391 --> 00:03:05.221
As for swimming, have you
ever seen a rabbit swim?

00:03:05.221 --> 00:03:07.432
(laughter)

00:03:07.432 --> 00:03:09.256
While it is important for us to know

00:03:09.256 --> 00:03:11.073
what we are not good at,

00:03:11.073 --> 00:03:13.322
we must cherish what is good in us.

00:03:13.322 --> 00:03:15.400
It's very fundamental to life.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.041
That is because it is
only on our strengths

00:03:18.041 --> 00:03:20.919
that can give us the energy
to correct our weaknesses.

00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:22.874
To make changes in any organization,

00:03:22.874 --> 00:03:26.541
you have to have a measure
of very high persistence.

00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:29.218
You've got to go on,
and on, and on, and on.

00:03:29.218 --> 00:03:30.664
If we don't have that persistency,

00:03:30.664 --> 00:03:32.121
very often you fall out

00:03:32.121 --> 00:03:34.422
just when success is around the corner.

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:36.817
The customer is a great source for ideas.

00:03:36.817 --> 00:03:39.213
The customer can actually participate

00:03:39.213 --> 00:03:41.549
in his getting better satisfaction,

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:43.607
provided we can listen to the customer

00:03:43.607 --> 00:03:45.511
and what he or she has to say.

00:03:45.511 --> 00:03:47.442
And I think this is the
most important thing

00:03:47.442 --> 00:03:49.381
in terms of customer visits,

00:03:49.381 --> 00:03:50.978
in terms of customer research,

00:03:50.978 --> 00:03:53.328
is how do you get the customer

00:03:53.328 --> 00:03:55.742
to say things, or indicate things,

00:03:55.742 --> 00:03:59.549
or give nuances of things
which are really unsaid?

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:01.506
And very often the
customer himself or herself

00:04:01.506 --> 00:04:03.234
does not know about it,

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:04.961
but that is the source of truly

00:04:04.961 --> 00:04:07.696
very, very interesting ideas.

00:04:07.696 --> 00:04:09.330
My friend was sharing with me a story

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.440
of his eight year old niece.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.370
She would always complain
about the quality

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.089
of the breakfast and about
the quality of the food

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.184
she would get in the house.

00:04:18.184 --> 00:04:19.682
She came from a family
where there was a cook,

00:04:19.682 --> 00:04:24.204
and they tried just about
everything to please her.

00:04:24.204 --> 00:04:28.219
Finally, my friend took
the child to a supermarket

00:04:28.219 --> 00:04:32.197
and bought her one of these
ready-to-cook breakfasts.

00:04:32.197 --> 00:04:33.741
The child had to cut the packet

00:04:33.741 --> 00:04:36.094
and pour water in the dish.

00:04:36.094 --> 00:04:37.922
After that, it took two
minutes in the microwave

00:04:37.922 --> 00:04:39.677
to be ready.

00:04:39.677 --> 00:04:43.287
The child found the food
to be absolutely delicious.

00:04:43.287 --> 00:04:45.805
The difference was that she had cooked it

00:04:45.805 --> 00:04:48.040
with her own hands.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.797
She had put work and effort into it.

00:04:50.797 --> 00:04:54.159
I have found that nothing
gives as much satisfaction

00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:56.649
as earning our rewards.

00:04:56.649 --> 00:04:59.227
A rupee earned is much more valuable

00:04:59.227 --> 00:05:01.028
than five rupees found.

00:05:01.028 --> 00:05:03.277
I think the simplest thing
in dealing with disagreements

00:05:03.277 --> 00:05:04.460
is get them onto the table.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:05.624
(laughter)

00:05:05.624 --> 00:05:08.979
I think that nothing
breeds more discomfort,

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:11.034
and frustration, and anger with people

00:05:11.034 --> 00:05:13.467
if you bottle them up.

00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:15.469
That's very fundamental.

00:05:15.469 --> 00:05:16.911
And you don't always win

00:05:16.911 --> 00:05:18.640
when you get them onto the table,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.807
but at least you come to
certain points of understanding,

00:05:23.905 --> 00:05:26.031
which makes for people to move forward.

00:05:26.031 --> 00:05:29.923
And you know, we, to run
large leadership positions

00:05:29.923 --> 00:05:32.906
successfully, you can debate issues,

00:05:32.906 --> 00:05:34.752
you can get issues onto a table.

00:05:34.752 --> 00:05:38.400
But what you would expect is
that when a decision is made,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.387
people fall in line going forward.

00:05:41.387 --> 00:05:43.431
And if they have strong conviction

00:05:43.431 --> 00:05:45.405
against the decision made,

00:05:45.405 --> 00:05:47.321
it's best that they get out of the system

00:05:47.321 --> 00:05:49.243
and don't frustrate themselves

00:05:49.243 --> 00:05:52.195
and frustrate other
members of the colleagues

00:05:52.195 --> 00:05:54.583
with their frustration.

00:05:54.583 --> 00:05:56.227
As Gary Hamel puts it,

00:05:56.227 --> 00:05:58.515
businesses are good at getting better,

00:05:58.515 --> 00:06:01.545
but poor at getting different.

00:06:01.545 --> 00:06:03.930
It is very difficult for
successful organizations

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.376
to develop destructive innovations

00:06:06.376 --> 00:06:09.115
that would threaten the
basis of their success.

00:06:09.115 --> 00:06:11.802
Often they are put out of business

00:06:11.802 --> 00:06:15.327
when some similar company
or smaller company

00:06:15.327 --> 00:06:18.327
develops a radically new technology.

00:06:19.757 --> 00:06:22.419
Which employees working in
a booming telecom company

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:25.330
in the 1990s would have suggested

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.910
that free voiceover internet telephony

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.246
would be something that
they could develop?

00:06:31.246 --> 00:06:34.006
It took a start up Skype to make a success

00:06:34.006 --> 00:06:35.486
of this radical idea.

00:06:35.486 --> 00:06:37.771
- When you talk about
ethics and integrating

00:06:37.771 --> 00:06:39.279
in the Indian context,

00:06:39.279 --> 00:06:41.809
the two companies that come to mind are,

00:06:41.809 --> 00:06:44.336
one is (mumbles) and
the other is (mumbles).

00:06:44.336 --> 00:06:46.527
What is so special about
these two companies

00:06:46.527 --> 00:06:49.260
that has been imbibed
in these organizations?

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:51.051
How is it done?

00:06:51.051 --> 00:06:54.331
- Very simple. It's done by practice.

00:06:54.331 --> 00:06:57.175
There's no other way you imbibe things

00:06:57.175 --> 00:06:58.146
in an organization.

00:06:58.146 --> 00:06:58.979
It is practice.

00:06:58.979 --> 00:06:59.968
Integrity is practice.

00:06:59.968 --> 00:07:01.261
Leadership is practice.

00:07:01.261 --> 00:07:03.836
Innovation is practice.

00:07:03.836 --> 00:07:05.426
It's all practice.

00:07:05.426 --> 00:07:07.602
You got to figure out what
you want your company to be

00:07:07.602 --> 00:07:09.298
and what you consider is non-compromisable

00:07:09.298 --> 00:07:10.834
and not negotiable.

00:07:10.834 --> 00:07:12.241
And then send those out,

00:07:12.241 --> 00:07:14.197
and keep them as simple as possible,

00:07:14.197 --> 00:07:15.583
and keep them as few as possible,

00:07:15.583 --> 00:07:17.307
and practice them.

00:07:17.307 --> 00:07:20.529
And practice them every
day, every hour, ever minute

00:07:20.529 --> 00:07:22.892
and have zero exception to it.

00:07:22.892 --> 00:07:24.574
(mumbles) advice to
students who want to start

00:07:24.574 --> 00:07:26.147
an organization like Wipro?

00:07:26.147 --> 00:07:26.980
- Yes, sir.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:28.780
- You know, the reality is
you have to start small.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:29.613
(laughter)

00:07:29.613 --> 00:07:30.962
Like we started small.

00:07:30.962 --> 00:07:34.855
And you must have a
very clear understanding

00:07:34.855 --> 00:07:37.375
of why you want to do it

00:07:37.375 --> 00:07:40.897
and what focus you want to build.

00:07:40.897 --> 00:07:42.063
And the focus you want to build

00:07:42.063 --> 00:07:43.937
in terms of starting an organization

00:07:43.937 --> 00:07:47.633
is something which must be
deeply passionate inside you.

00:07:47.633 --> 00:07:49.751
Because you're going to
go through ups and downs.

00:07:49.751 --> 00:07:52.014
In many cases, you're going to fail.

00:07:52.014 --> 00:07:54.692
Certainly in parts of your journey,

00:07:54.692 --> 00:07:57.528
you're going to have major failures.

00:07:57.528 --> 00:07:59.968
So unless you have a very clear commitment

00:07:59.968 --> 00:08:01.741
to the focus which you build

00:08:01.741 --> 00:08:04.087
in terms of what do you
want to build successfully,

00:08:04.087 --> 00:08:07.272
and have the flexibility
to change that focus,

00:08:07.272 --> 00:08:10.394
because you've not judged
the market properly.

00:08:10.394 --> 00:08:12.071
The markets don't run after you, you know.

00:08:12.071 --> 00:08:14.541
You have to run after
what the market demand is,

00:08:14.541 --> 00:08:17.271
what the market demand you can create.

00:08:17.271 --> 00:08:19.360
But you have to be persistent,

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.286
and you have to be prepared
to make sacrifices,

00:08:22.286 --> 00:08:24.332
because you will have to make sacrifices

00:08:24.332 --> 00:08:27.716
in the early stages, and
some very major sacrifices.

00:08:27.716 --> 00:08:30.224
And most of them will
be financial sacrifices.

00:08:30.224 --> 00:08:31.892
Because you won't get cashflow

00:08:31.892 --> 00:08:33.937
the way you hope to get cashflow,

00:08:33.937 --> 00:08:36.380
unless you have a brilliant, genius idea,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.170
then you'll get cashflow.

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:39.352
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:42.502
I made this video
because PRNV asked me to.

00:08:42.502 --> 00:08:43.644
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:08:43.644 --> 00:08:45.108
that you want me to profile,

00:08:45.108 --> 00:08:46.357
leave it in the comments below,

00:08:46.357 --> 00:08:47.473
and I'll see what I can do.

00:08:47.473 --> 00:08:49.208
I'd also love to know
which of the top 10 rules

00:08:49.208 --> 00:08:51.012
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:08:51.012 --> 00:08:52.021
Leave it in the comments,

00:08:52.021 --> 00:08:53.575
and I will join in the discussion.

00:08:53.575 --> 00:08:54.799
Thank you so much for watching.

00:08:54.799 --> 00:08:57.690
Continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is,

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.523
and I'll see you soon.

